NAME
====

**wattle-applications** - how Wattle applications are laid out, built and installed

DESCRIPTION
===========

An application is a directory containing an _info file_, source files (in either
Wattle or Zig) and documentation. `wattle-apps(1)` installs an application and
`wattle-build(1)` builds the artifacts that an application declares. This
page describes what an application contains and where its files are installed.

VENDORING
=========

Vendoring a dependency refers to the practice of copying into the source tree of
an application the Wattle source files that the application uses. Within the
code, vendored dependencies are imported using a relative import:

````
(import ./vendor/greetings)
````

Wattle's philosophy is that dependencies that are Wattle source files should be
part of the application. A vendored copy is the version the application was
tested with, it changes only when the application's author changes it and the
application runs without any dependency needing to be installed.

This has trade-offs. An application includes a copy of each dependency and a
fix to a dependency reaches the application only when its author updates the
copy. Wattle takes the view that an application's author is, most of the time,
in the best position to weigh those trade-offs. Because of this
`wattle-apps(1)` does not provide a mechanism to install Wattle source
libraries.

The application lists what it vendors under ``:vendored`` in its info file, and
``wattle deps vendor`` copies each entry into the application. See
`wattle-deps(1)`. An entry without a ``:ref`` uses the HEAD of the default
branch of a Git repository each time.

LAYOUT
======

An application may contain these files:

- ``info.edn``:
  The info file, at the root of the application.

- ``hooks/init.wattle`` or ``hooks.wattle``:
  The application script. If both exist, ``hooks/init.wattle`` is used. The
  rest of ``hooks`` is installed with it, so the script can import the files
  beside it.

An application needs an application script or an info file that declares
``:artifacts`` or ``:man``.

INFO FILE
=========

The info file holds one map. All keys are optional, but ``:name`` and
``:group`` are needed to install an application:

- ``:name``:
  The name of the application. It is one directory name and is unique across
  groups.

- ``:group``:
  The group of the application, by convention a reverse domain such as
  ``net.inqk``. It is one directory name. A directory name is not empty, is not
  ``.`` or ``..`` and contains no path separator.

- ``:vendored``:
  A vector of maps that describe the sources that ``wattle deps vendor`` copies
  into the application. Each has a ``:name`` and a ``:url``, and may have a
  ``:ref``, a ``:prefix`` and ``:paths``. See VENDORING above and
  `wattle-deps(1)`.

- ``:artifacts``:
  A vector of maps that describe what ``wattle build`` builds. Each has a
  ``:type`` and a ``:name``. See ``Declarations`` in `wattle-build(1)`.

- ``:man``:
  A vector of the paths of man pages. The section is the digit that ends the
  file name, so ``man/greet.1`` is installed in ``man1``.

Example:

````
{:name "greeter"
 :group "net.inqk"
 :vendored [{:name "greetings"
             :url "https://example.com/greetings.git"
             :prefix "deps/greetings"}]
 :artifacts [{:type :exe
              :name "hello"
              :entry "main.wattle"}]
 :man ["man/hello.1"]}
````

INSTALLATION
============

An application with an application script is installed by running the hooks
that the script defines, and nothing else. An application without one is
installed from its info file: each ``:exe`` artifact is built with
`wattle-build(1)` and its program is installed in ``bin``, and each file in
``:man`` is installed in ``share/man``. A ``:lib`` artifact is installed only
by an application script, and a ``:web`` artifact is not installed.

Hooks
-----

The application script defines a function for each hook it uses. Each takes the
application's manifest. The hooks run in this order at installation:

1. ``clean``, only when a clean build is requested
2. ``build``
3. ``install``
4. ``check``, only when a check is requested

The ``remove`` hook runs when the application is removed.

Within a hook, these functions add files to the manifest, so that
``wattle apps remove`` can remove them:

- ``app/add-bin``:
  Adds a program to the binpath.

- ``app/add-manpage``:
  Adds a man page to the manpath.

Example of ``hooks/init.wattle``:

````
(defn install
  [manifest &]
  (app/add-bin manifest "hello")
  (app/add-manpage manifest "hello.1"))
````

INSTALLED FILES
===============

All locations are under ``*prefix*``; see ``WATTLE_PREFIX`` in `wattle(1)`.

- ``lib/wattle``:
  The libpath, which holds the applications. ``import`` does not search it.
  See ``MODULES`` in `wattle(7)`.

- ``lib/wattle/apps/<group>/<name>``:
  The directory of an installed application: ``manifest.edn``, which lists its
  files, a copy of its info file and a copy of its application script, with
  the rest of its ``hooks`` directory.

- ``bin``:
  The binpath, for programs.

- ``share/man``:
  The manpath, for man pages.

